# Docker Environment

The Help Desk project uses Docker to provide an isolated and reproducible development environment.

The application is divided into three main services:

```text
Docker Compose
├── helpdesk-nginx
├── helpdesk-php
└── helpdesk-mysql
```

## Services

### Nginx

The `helpdesk-nginx` container works as the web server and receives HTTP requests from the browser.

It forwards PHP requests to the PHP container.

### PHP

The `helpdesk-php` container runs the PHP application using PHP-FPM.

The application code is executed inside this container.

### MySQL

The `helpdesk-mysql` container provides the MySQL database used by the Help Desk application.

## Project Structure

```text
Help_desk/
├── app/
│   ├── Controllers/
│   ├── Models/
│   ├── Services/
│   └── Views/
├── config/
├── database/
├── docker/
│   ├── nginx/
│   │   └── default.conf
│   └── php/
│       └── Dockerfile
├── document/
├── public/
│   └── assets/
├── routes/
├── storage/
├── tests/
├── vendor/
├── compose.yaml
├── Dockerfile
├── composer.json
├── composer.lock
├── index.php
└── README.md
```

## Starting the Environment

From the project root directory, run:

```bash
docker compose up -d
```

This command starts the required containers in the background.

To check the running containers:

```bash
docker ps
```

The following containers should be running:

```text
helpdesk-nginx
helpdesk-php
helpdesk-mysql
```

## Stopping the Environment

To stop the project's containers, run:

```bash
docker compose down
```

This stops and removes the containers created by Docker Compose.

## Application Flow

The request flow is organized as follows:

```text
Browser
   ↓
Nginx
   ↓
PHP-FPM
   ↓
Help Desk Application
   ↓
MySQL
```

Nginx handles incoming HTTP requests and forwards PHP requests to PHP-FPM. The PHP application processes the request and communicates with MySQL when database access is required.

## Development Environment

Docker allows the same environment to be reproduced across different machines, reducing the need to manually install and configure PHP, Nginx, and MySQL.

This is especially useful for development teams and for companies that want to deploy the Help Desk in their own infrastructure.

## Production

Before deploying the system to production, review the Docker configuration, environment variables, database credentials, network configuration, security settings, and persistent storage.

Development configurations should not be used directly in a production environment without proper review.
